            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.576,
                0.425,
                0.846,
                0.442
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Matterhorn (Switzerland)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.458,
            0.857,
            0.479
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The volume V of a pyramidal shape is found by using the formula"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.343,
            0.481,
            0.66,
            0.515
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_12_equation_9.jpg",
          "content": "V = \\frac{1}{3} \\times  area of phase  \\times  height ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.516,
            0.881,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The same formula is applicable whether the base is triangular, square or any other shaped flat surface."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.556,
            0.882,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that the formula is the same as the one used for the volume of a cone. Indeed, we may think of a cone as a pyramid with a circular base."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.594,
            0.882,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The surface area of a pyramidal shape is found by adding up the areas of all the polygonal faces. So, we do not need a separate formula for the surface area."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.677,
            0.546,
            0.699
          ],
